              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Arabic for a trainee, then the Arabic for to practise."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: اعتاد",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "اعتاد — iʿtāda — \"to get used to\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "اعتادت الممرضة على العمل في الليل. iʿtādat al-mumarriḍa ʿalā al-ʿamal fī al-layl. The nurse got used to working at night."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Present: يعتاد, yaʿtād."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It takes على, or a direct object."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "اعتاد (iʿtāda) is built on the native Arabic root ʿ-w-d, \"to return\" (Lane). ʿĀda, a habit, which you know, is from the same root: a thing that keeps coming back."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
